    "innovation": "This collection of letters presents a scientific debate on the speed and economic consequences of methane release from the thawing Arctic, questioning a scenario of a sudden release and highlighting the uneven distribution of warming's costs and benefits.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "sudden release of 50 gigatonnes of methane",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Arctic Ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Gail Whiteman and colleagues suggest that the opening up of the Arctic Ocean could bring more economic costs than benefits, owing to climatic effects resulting from a sudden release of 50 gigatonnes of methane from the area (Nature 499, 401\u2013403; 2013).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "methane release",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Arctic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "However, our literature review of the impact of sea-ice decline on Arctic greenhouse-gas exchange indicates that methane release is likely to be more gradual because of a slow rate of heat penetration into the subsea permafrost (see F. J. W. Parmentier et al. Nature Clim. Change 3, 195\u2013202; 2013).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "elevated methane concentrations",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "East Siberian Arctic Shelf",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Although elevated methane concentrations have been observed in water and the atmosphere above the East Siberian Arctic Shelf, it is not clear whether these have been caused by recent warming or by natural processes linked to glacial\u2013interglacial changes (V. V. Petrenko et al. Science 329, 1146\u20131147; 2010).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "large and sudden release of methane",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "East Siberian Arctic Shelf",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "We disagree with Gail Whiteman and colleagues that there is \u201clikely\u201d to be a large and sudden release of methane from the East Siberian Arctic Shelf (Nature 499, 401\u2013403; 2013).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "accelerating sea-ice retreat",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Arctic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Gail Whiteman, Chris Hope and Peter Wadhams respond: Since 2005, accelerating sea-ice retreat in the Arctic has exposed the sea bed to much warmer conditions than those considered in earlier studies that anticipated a slow release of methane.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "damage from Arctic warming",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Arctic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The Madrid-based humanitarian organization DARA expects the cost of this damage in the Arctic to reach US$80 billion annually by 2030 (see go.nature.com/vnlzax).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "losses from Arctic warming",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Russia",
            "target_entity_type": "Country",
            "paragraph": "These losses will be unevenly distributed, with more than 90% likely to occur in Russia.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Arctic warming",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Arctic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "We believe that the uneven distribution of costs and benefits of Arctic warming calls for a new approach to governance in the region. This should ensure equitable compensation and support for those affected most.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
    ],
